+# Full study: the square lower bound for 2-increasing triples
+
+## Frozen target
+
+The frozen declaration is
+
+```lean
+theorem maximalLength_ge_of_isSquare {n : ℕ} (h : IsSquare n) :
+    n.sqrt ^ 3 ≤ maximalLength n
+```
+
+Here `maximalLength n` is the supremum of the lengths of lists of triples of
+natural numbers whose coordinates lie in `[1,n]` and in which every earlier
+triple is strictly smaller than every later triple in at least two of the three
+coordinates.  The proof below is unconditional apart from the declaration's
+explicit premise that `n` is a square.
+
+## Anchored reconstruction
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-00:BEGIN -->
+The root goal is to exhibit one admissible list of length `sqrt(n)^3`; because
+`maximalLength n` is the supremum of all admissible lengths, membership of that
+one length in the defining set is enough once the set is shown bounded.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-00:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-01:BEGIN -->
+From `IsSquare n` choose `m` with `n = m*m`.  Mathlib's exact natural-square
+identity `Nat.sqrt_eq m` then rewrites `sqrt(m*m)` to `m`, reducing the target
+to `m^3 ≤ maximalLength (m*m)`.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-01:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-02:BEGIN -->
+For each `0 ≤ i,j,k < m`, define
+`T(i,j,k) = (i*m+j+1, i*m+k+1, j*m+k+1)`.  Let `S_m` be the list of all these
+triples in lexicographic order on `(i,j,k)`, implemented by three nested
+`List.range` traversals.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-02:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-03:BEGIN -->
+If two indices first differ at `k`, then `i` and `j` agree and `k < k'`.
+Consequently the second coordinates `i*m+k+1` and the third coordinates
+`j*m+k+1` both strictly increase, so the corresponding triples are
+2-increasing.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-03:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-04:BEGIN -->
+If two indices first differ at `j`, then `i` agrees and `j < j'`.  The first
+coordinate increases directly.  The third coordinate increases because
+`j*m+k < (j+1)*m ≤ j'*m ≤ j'*m+k'`, using `k < m`.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-04:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-05:BEGIN -->
+If two indices first differ at `i`, then `i < i'`.  Both the first and second
+coordinates increase: for either trailing digit `c < m`, the base-`m`
+inequality `i*m+c < (i+1)*m ≤ i'*m ≤ i'*m+d` applies independently of the
+later trailing digit `d`.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-05:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-06:BEGIN -->
+The three first-difference cases are exhaustive for lexicographically ordered
+distinct triples of digits.  `List.pairwise_flatMap` composes the inner `k`,
+middle `j`, and outer `i` arguments, yielding `IsIncreasingTwo S_m` for the
+entire nested list rather than merely for adjacent entries.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-06:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-07:BEGIN -->
+Every coordinate of `T(i,j,k)` is at least one.  For the upper bound, if
+`a,b < m`, then `a*m+b < m*m`; therefore `a*m+b+1 ≤ m*m`.  Applying this to
+the digit pairs `(i,j)`, `(i,k)`, and `(j,k)` puts the full range of every
+constructed triple inside `Set.Icc 1 (m*m)`.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-07:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-08:BEGIN -->
+Each of the three nested `List.range m` traversals has length `m`, and each
+choice produces exactly one triple.  The flat-map and map length formulas thus
+give `S_m.length = m*m*m = m^3`.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-08:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-09:BEGIN -->
+The set of admissible lengths is bounded.  For `n ≥ 2`, a list longer than
+`n^2` would contain two entries with the same first two coordinates by the
+pigeonhole principle; whichever occurs first cannot be strictly smaller in
+two coordinates, contradicting pairwise 2-increase.  For `n=0` the range
+condition forces the list empty, and for `n=1` pairwise 2-increase permits at
+most one all-ones triple.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-09:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-10:BEGIN -->
+The coordinate-range lemma, pairwise lemma, and length identity place
+`S_m.length` in the exact set used by `maximalLength (m*m)`.  The bounded-set
+law `le_csSup` therefore yields `m^3 ≤ maximalLength (m*m)` with no appeal to
+the source's former `sorryAx` body.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-10:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-11:BEGIN -->
+Substituting the square witness and rewriting the natural square root completes
+the source-shaped theorem.  The canonical `Statement` is definitionally the
+same quantified proposition, so the checked `source_to_canonical` transport
+closes the package root `theorem_complete` without strengthening, weakening,
+or reordering any binder.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-11:END -->
+
+## Edge cases and trust boundary
+
+The construction handles `m=0` and `m=1` without separate root cases.  For
+`m=0`, it is empty and proves `0 ≤ F(0)`.  For `m=1`, it contains the single
+triple `(1,1,1)`.  All helper declarations have Lean bodies.  The proof does
+not import the original Formal Conjectures theorem and hence cannot inherit its
+`sorryAx`; it imports only the local exact statement module and pinned mathlib.
+
+The machine transcript reports Lean's standard logical primitives `propext`,
+`Classical.choice`, and `Quot.sound`, which enter through sets, finite
+pigeonhole reasoning, and the noncomputable natural supremum used by the frozen
+source definition.  No claim-specific axiom, bodyless oracle, unsafe
+declaration, `sorry`, or `admit` occurs.
